WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.710
Saving weights is enough for
only part of the problem.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.110
Alongside the scaler and feature order,
weights reproduce a forward prediction.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.970
They do not necessarily reproduce
the next training update.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.790
Consider a four row fixture
with batches of three.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.470
After one update,
one row remains in the current epoch.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.950
The next batch must
contain that exact row,

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.790
and its mean gradient has denominator one.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.930
Starting a new permutation now
would skip the intended remainder.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.590
Momentum also needs the
accumulated velocity.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.150
Adam needs both moments
and its update counter.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:43.670
We use this small mid epoch pause
to test continuation precisely,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.290
without claiming a hosted or large
training recovery has been demonstrated.

00:00:50.525 --> 00:00:54.335
The continuation stores parameters
and optimizer configuration.

00:00:55.325 --> 00:00:59.105
The first and second buffers preserve
the history that determines the next

00:00:59.105 --> 00:00:59.715
direction,

00:01:00.145 --> 00:01:02.755
and the counter preserves
Adam bias correction.

00:01:03.665 --> 00:01:07.855
The stream saves its current permutation,
cursor and epoch,

00:01:08.285 --> 00:01:10.815
plus generator state
for future permutations.

00:01:11.625 --> 00:01:16.615
An epoch seed alone is insufficient
if its generator has already advanced.

00:01:17.165 --> 00:01:21.375
Examples seen and schedule
settings determine the next rate.

00:01:22.065 --> 00:01:24.435
Source, lock, data, schema,

00:01:25.165 --> 00:01:29.605
feature order and scaler identities
establish which calculation this state

00:01:29.605 --> 00:01:30.515
belongs to.

00:01:30.925 --> 00:01:33.235
Outer run records retain those identities,

00:01:33.665 --> 00:01:37.895
while the low level continuation validates
its configuration and dimensions.

00:01:38.685 --> 00:01:42.945
A valid paused state is explicitly
distinguished from a failed numerical

00:01:42.945 --> 00:01:43.835
trial.

00:01:46.125 --> 00:01:49.395
The fixture first pauses after one update.

00:01:49.885 --> 00:01:53.015
It serializes and reloads
the state through JSON,

00:01:53.365 --> 00:01:58.815
exercising the saved representation rather
than passing a live object in memory.

00:01:59.525 --> 00:02:03.215
Resumed training continues to
the original example budget.

00:02:03.765 --> 00:02:08.905
A separate uninterrupted run uses
the same configuration and seed.

00:02:08.905 --> 00:02:11.255
The check compares exact parameters,

00:02:11.645 --> 00:02:12.195
moments,

00:02:12.605 --> 00:02:14.295
counters, batch order,

00:02:14.545 --> 00:02:18.895
generator state and schedule
progress for all three update rules.

00:02:19.325 --> 00:02:23.455
For this small same environment fixture,
they agree exactly.

00:02:24.405 --> 00:02:29.255
Wall clock duration is recorded separately
and naturally differs between executions.

00:02:30.245 --> 00:02:34.025
These checks establish a local
continuation contract at a mid epoch

00:02:34.025 --> 00:02:35.015
boundary.

00:02:35.405 --> 00:02:37.815
They do not imply a remote runtime,

00:02:38.085 --> 00:02:41.835
device change or general training
platform has passed recovery.

00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:48.597
The earlier scalar Adam example shows
why this is more than bookkeeping.

00:02:49.087 --> 00:02:52.117
After gradients plus two then minus one,

00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:56.137
the retained first moment remains
positive at the second update.

00:02:56.467 --> 00:03:00.814
Continuing with that history takes the
parameter from approximately negative zero

00:03:00.814 --> 00:03:04.577
point one to negative zero
point one two six six.

00:03:05.007 --> 00:03:10.227
Resetting both buffers and calling the
next step t equals one would respond to

00:03:10.227 --> 00:03:12.817
the latest negative
gradient as a fresh start.

00:03:13.127 --> 00:03:14.797
It would take a different direction.

00:03:15.287 --> 00:03:15.917
Likewise,

00:03:16.147 --> 00:03:20.897
restarting a decay schedule at zero
examples would enlarge the next rate,

00:03:21.207 --> 00:03:24.057
and regenerating the stream
would change its rows.

00:03:24.607 --> 00:03:28.317
A plausible looking training curve
cannot reveal every such reset.

00:03:28.827 --> 00:03:32.737
Exact continuation checks make those
hidden dependencies observable.

00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:39.697
A failed numerical candidate remains
useful evidence about the chosen rate,

00:03:39.967 --> 00:03:42.737
but it is not a successful
continuation checkpoint.

00:03:43.387 --> 00:03:46.087
The saved state marks
whether it is resumable,

00:03:46.087 --> 00:03:48.557
and loading failed state is rejected.

00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:51.437
The run also retains why it stopped:

00:03:52.047 --> 00:03:55.397
example budget, update budget,
time limit, pause,

00:03:55.827 --> 00:03:56.357
divergence

00:03:56.807 --> 00:03:58.357
or nonfinite calculation.

00:03:59.047 --> 00:04:02.657
The implementation now has
sixty passing source checks,

00:04:03.047 --> 00:04:07.537
independent metric and identity audits,
and source and wheel verification.

00:04:08.387 --> 00:04:10.257
These establish checked behavior.

00:04:10.647 --> 00:04:13.017
They do not establish
learner understanding,

00:04:13.347 --> 00:04:15.997
final test performance or hosted recovery.

00:04:16.647 --> 00:04:20.417
The private notes contain the complete
calculations and result comparisons.

00:04:21.287 --> 00:04:26.157
Before the next objective, explain which
saved item determines the next row,

00:04:26.427 --> 00:04:29.017
next learning rate and
next Adam correction.

00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:32.677
That connects the code to the
stateful model being learned.

